  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1010 PM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nUL high is centered over the Four Corners, and dry air has\ncirculated into the area from the east and northeast. PW values\nare under an 1\" while dewpoints are mostly in the 40s.\nAdditionally, the presence of the high has brought warming\nthroughout the column, further reducing instability. The result\nhas been a significant downturn for convection with only a few \nstorms forming in the CWA this afternoon. We will pretty much \nremain in this pattern through Tuesday with mainly the Gila and \nfar western lowlands having a chance for storms. Highs will \ncontinue to be a few degrees above normal with most lowlands near \nor just above 100.\n\nBy Wednesday, we will see a fairly substantial pattern change. \nThe high over the Four Corners will rapidly weaken with the \ndominant high shifting across the Southern Plains, extending \nacross the Gulf Coast. This will open the CWA to southerly flow \nwith a subtropical tap of moisture--a more classic monsoonal \npattern. Shower and thunderstorm chances will increase for the \nlast half of the week though the NBM is playing conservative in \nthe lowlands, but given pattern recognition there should be at \nleast be isolated coverage in the lowlands given PW values over \nan inch. Given the increased moisture and the departure of the \nhigh, temperatures should come down at least a little as well.",
